  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, which builds the island scene in Aura3D. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't build or run it here, so I haven't seen a rendered frame.\n\n- **Island:** a flat-shaded low-poly island built from code. Each face gets its own normal and a colour by height and slope: sand, grass, dark rock and grey peaks. Vertices are jittered slightly. About 26 trees (a trunk plus two stacked cones) and about 14 rocks sit on it, placed at random heights on the terrain and drawn as instanced meshes.\n- **Water:** a 260-unit wave grid driven by a custom shader. It animates through the `waveClock` uniform, and flat facets come from screen-space derivatives. It has a depth tint that is lighter near the island, a fresnel mix toward the sky colour, a sun or moon glint and brighter wave crests. Far water fades into the horizon colour.\n- **Day/night cycle:** a full cycle takes 20 seconds and starts at hour 9. A Preetham sky with stars follows the sun. Each frame the sun and moon directional lights move, the sun's colour and intensity change, and the hemisphere ambient light is scaled. Ambient never drops below 0.45 and the moon keeps a minimum, so the scene never goes black. The water uniforms for sky, deep and shallow colours, light direction and brightness follow the same cycle.\n- **Camera:** an orbit camera at [0, 16, 31] looks at the island and auto-rotates at 0.12 rad/s.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set on the first `requestAnimationFrame` after `app.ready()`.\n\nI'm unsure about the horizon. The water's far-edge fade colour is my own approximation of the sky's horizon colour, so a faint band may show where the water ends.\n\n",
